Encapsulation means hiding internal state and exposing only a controlled interface, so the object can protect its own invariants. In C# you use private fields, properties with restricted setters (private set, init), and methods that validate changes instead of public mutable fields.
```csharp
public class BankAccount {
    public decimal Balance { get; private set; }
    public void Deposit(decimal amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new ArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        Balance += amount;
    }
}
```

Deep inheritance hierarchies create tight coupling: a change in the base class can break every subclass (the fragile base class problem). Subclasses often inherit behaviour they do not need, and combinations of features cause class explosion (FlyingSwimmingDuck). With composition you combine small behaviours through interfaces, change them at runtime and test each in isolation.
```csharp
public class Duck {
    private readonly IFlyBehavior _fly;
    public Duck(IFlyBehavior fly) => _fly = fly;
    public void Fly() => _fly.Fly();
}
```

Define an interface with one method that picks a server, then implement each algorithm as its own class. The load balancer depends only on the interface, so strategies can be swapped via configuration or DI.
```csharp
public interface ILoadBalancingStrategy {
    Server Select(IReadOnlyList<Server> servers);
}
public class RoundRobinStrategy : ILoadBalancingStrategy {
    private int _index = -1;
    public Server Select(IReadOnlyList<Server> servers) {
        int i = Interlocked.Increment(ref _index);
        return servers[(i & int.MaxValue) % servers.Count];
    }
}
```

Replace the switch with an interface where each customer type is its own class, and register them in DI or a dictionary. Adding a new type now means adding a class, not editing the calculator.
```csharp
public interface IDiscountPolicy {
    string CustomerType { get; }
    decimal Apply(decimal amount);
}
public class PremiumDiscount : IDiscountPolicy {
    public string CustomerType => "Premium";
    public decimal Apply(decimal amount) => amount * 0.9m;
}
```
The calculator receives IEnumerable<IDiscountPolicy> and picks by CustomerType.

High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions, and abstractions should not depend on details. In practice an OrderService depends on IOrderRepository, not SqlOrderRepository. ASP.NET Core's built-in DI container wires the concrete implementation at startup, so business logic stays testable and swappable.
```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderRepository, SqlOrderRepository>();
public class OrderService(IOrderRepository repo) { }
```

Introduce an INotificationChannel interface; each channel is a class, and the dispatcher loops over injected channels. Adding a channel means adding a class and a DI registration.
```csharp
public interface INotificationChannel {
    string Name { get; }
    Task SendAsync(Notification n, CancellationToken ct);
}
public class NotificationDispatcher(IEnumerable<INotificationChannel> channels) {
    public Task SendAsync(Notification n, CancellationToken ct) =>
        Task.WhenAll(channels.Where(c => n.Channels.Contains(c.Name))
                             .Select(c => c.SendAsync(n, ct)));
}
```

Lazy<T> is the simplest correct approach: it is thread-safe by default and creates the instance only on first access.
```csharp
public sealed class AppConfig {
    private static readonly Lazy<AppConfig> _instance =
        new(() => new AppConfig());
    public static AppConfig Instance => _instance.Value;
    private AppConfig() { /* load settings */ }
}
```
sealed prevents subclasses from creating extra instances.

Register every processor in DI and let the factory pick by key, so new processors only need a new class and registration.
```csharp
public interface IPaymentProcessor {
    string Method { get; }
    Task PayAsync(decimal amount);
}
public class PaymentProcessorFactory(IEnumerable<IPaymentProcessor> processors) {
    private readonly Dictionary<string, IPaymentProcessor> _map =
        processors.ToDictionary(p => p.Method, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    public IPaymentProcessor Get(string method) =>
        _map.TryGetValue(method, out var p) ? p
            : throw new NotSupportedException(method);
}
```

Each method sets a value and returns the builder so calls can be chained; Build validates and creates the immutable object.
```csharp
public class RequestBuilder {
    private string _url = ""; private string _method = "GET";
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _headers = new();
    public RequestBuilder Url(string url) { _url = url; return this; }
    public RequestBuilder Method(string m) { _method = m; return this; }
    public RequestBuilder Header(string k, string v) { _headers[k] = v; return this; }
    public Request Build() => string.IsNullOrEmpty(_url)
        ? throw new InvalidOperationException("Url required")
        : new Request(_url, _method, _headers);
}
```

The interface maps a key to a shard index; the router uses whichever strategy is configured.
```csharp
public interface IShardKeyStrategy {
    int GetShard(string key, int shardCount);
}
public class HashShardStrategy : IShardKeyStrategy {
    public int GetShard(string key, int shardCount) {
        uint h = 2166136261;
        foreach (char c in key) h = (h ^ c) * 16777619;
        return (int)(h % (uint)shardCount);
    }
}
```
A RangeShardStrategy would look up the key in a sorted list of range boundaries. Note string.GetHashCode is randomised per process, so use a stable hash.

The interface lives with the domain; the implementation lives in the infrastructure layer.
```csharp
public interface IOrderRepository {
    Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct);
    Task AddAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct);
}
public class EfOrderRepository(AppDbContext db) : IOrderRepository {
    public Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct) =>
        db.Orders.Include(o => o.Items).FirstOrDefaultAsync(o => o.Id == id, ct);
    public async Task AddAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct) =>
        await db.Orders.AddAsync(order, ct);
}
```

Put the orchestration in a LoanService and keep rules in the domain objects or pluggable rule classes, so new rules do not change the service.
```csharp
public Loan Checkout(Member member, BookItem item) {
    if (member.ActiveLoans.Count >= MaxBooks)
        throw new DomainException("Loan limit reached");
    if (member.HasPendingFines) throw new DomainException("Pay fines first");
    item.MarkLoaned(); // throws if not Available
    var loan = new Loan(item, member, _clock.Today, _clock.Today.AddDays(14));
    member.AddLoan(loan);
    _loans.Add(loan);
    return loan;
}
```

Store LinkedListNode references in the dictionary so moving and removing are O(1).
```csharp
public class LruCache<K, V>(int capacity) where K : notnull {
    private readonly Dictionary<K, LinkedListNode<(K Key, V Val)>> _map = new();
    private readonly LinkedList<(K Key, V Val)> _list = new();
    public bool TryGet(K key, out V val) {
        if (!_map.TryGetValue(key, out var n)) { val = default!; return false; }
        _list.Remove(n); _list.AddFirst(n); val = n.Value.Val; return true;
    }
    public void Put(K key, V val) {
        if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out var n)) _list.Remove(n);
        else if (_map.Count == capacity) { _map.Remove(_list.Last!.Value.Key); _list.RemoveLast(); }
        _map[key] = _list.AddFirst((key, val)); }
}
```
